1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,661
Every practice midterm turns dollars into a probability threshold.

2
00:00:04,661 --> 00:00:09,031
In this video, you'll learn the one formula that does it, why it works,

3
00:00:09,031 --> 00:00:10,938
and every way the exam uses it.

4
00:00:12,533 --> 00:00:15,266
Here's the setup from practice midterm one.

5
00:00:15,266 --> 00:00:20,477
YourCabs gets a booking, and the assigned driver might cancel, with probability p.

6
00:00:20,477 --> 00:00:25,370
YourCabs can keep the driver, or reassign the booking to a more reliable one.

7
00:00:26,153 --> 00:00:31,733
If they keep the driver and the driver cancels, they lose 100 dollars.

8
00:00:31,733 --> 00:00:34,682
If the driver shows up, they earn 25.

9
00:00:34,682 --> 00:00:37,472
So keeping is worth 25 minus 125 p.

10
00:00:38,253 --> 00:00:44,628
If they reassign, the new driver almost never cancels: one time in a thousand,

11
00:00:44,628 --> 00:00:46,263
costing 110 dollars.

12
00:00:46,263 --> 00:00:48,143
Otherwise they earn 15.

13
00:00:48,143 --> 00:00:52,801
That branch is worth 14.875 dollars, no matter what p is.

14
00:00:54,400 --> 00:00:58,460
Now plot each action's expected payoff against p.

15
00:00:58,460 --> 00:01:04,178
Keeping starts at 25 dollars and falls as cancellations get likelier.

16
00:01:04,178 --> 00:01:06,664
Reassigning is flat at 14.875.

17
00:01:07,420 --> 00:01:14,241
Set them equal: 25 minus 125 p equals 14.875 gives p* equals 0.081.

18
00:01:14,241 --> 00:01:18,415
Below that, keeping the driver pays more.

19
00:01:18,415 --> 00:01:20,349
Above it, reassign.

20
00:01:20,349 --> 00:01:23,505
That's the whole decision rule.

21
00:01:25,100 --> 00:01:29,354
There's a faster way that works on every exam: think in regrets.

22
00:01:29,354 --> 00:01:31,415
First, name the positive class.

23
00:01:31,415 --> 00:01:33,143
Here, it's a cancellation.

24
00:01:33,143 --> 00:01:37,796
Predicting positive means taking the action you'd want for a positive,

25
00:01:37,796 --> 00:01:39,192
which is reassigning.

26
00:01:39,953 --> 00:01:47,364
If the driver really would cancel, reassigning beats keeping by 14.875 minus minus

27
00:01:47,364 --> 00:01:49,352
100, which is 114.875.

28
00:01:49,352 --> 00:01:55,317
Keeping in that world is a false negative, so that regret is L_FN.

29
00:01:56,087 --> 00:02:02,500
If the driver would show up, keeping beats reassigning by 25 minus 14.875,

30
00:02:02,500 --> 00:02:03,887
which is 10.125.

31
00:02:03,887 --> 00:02:09,260
Reassigning in that world is a false positive, so that's L_FP.

32
00:02:10,020 --> 00:02:19,592
Then the threshold is L_FP over L_FP plus L_FN: 10.125 over 125, which is 0.081.

33
00:02:19,592 --> 00:02:22,464
Same answer, no algebra.

34
00:02:24,067 --> 00:02:25,189
Why does it work?

35
00:02:25,189 --> 00:02:30,339
If you predict positive, you're only wrong when the case is actually negative.

36
00:02:30,339 --> 00:02:33,574
So your expected loss is one minus p, times L_FP.

37
00:02:34,353 --> 00:02:39,573
If you predict negative, you're only wrong when the case is actually positive.

38
00:02:39,573 --> 00:02:41,915
Your expected loss is p times L_FN.

39
00:02:42,687 --> 00:02:46,447
Predict positive when that first loss is smaller.

40
00:02:46,447 --> 00:02:52,050
Solve for p, and you get exactly p greater than L_FP over L_FP plus L_FN.

41
00:02:52,820 --> 00:02:57,732
Notice that multiplying both losses by the same number cancels out.

42
00:02:57,732 --> 00:02:59,419
Only the ratio matters.

43
00:02:59,419 --> 00:03:05,431
That's why practice midterm one can give CART losses of 11.35 and 1 instead of the

44
00:03:05,431 --> 00:03:06,530
dollar amounts.

45
00:03:08,133 --> 00:03:12,778
Here's the formula across all three practice midterms.

46
00:03:12,778 --> 00:03:19,659
Question seven on midterm one gives L_FN of 2 and L_FP of 1, so p* is one third.

47
00:03:19,659 --> 00:03:22,669
YourCabs, as we just saw, is 0.081.

48
00:03:23,453 --> 00:03:27,447
For Lending Club, the positive class is a default.

49
00:03:27,447 --> 00:03:31,361
Funding a loan that defaults costs 4,000 dollars,

50
00:03:31,361 --> 00:03:34,636
and rejecting a good loan gives up 1,000.

51
00:03:34,636 --> 00:03:37,831
So p* is 1,000 over 5,000, which is 0.2.

52
00:03:38,587 --> 00:03:42,248
And for the golf bet on midterm three, p* is two thirds.

53
00:03:42,248 --> 00:03:43,621
That one has a twist.

54
00:03:45,233 --> 00:03:51,326
On practice midterm three, you pay 100 dollars for a bet that returns 150 if a

55
00:03:51,326 --> 00:03:54,607
golfer wins more than 2.5 million dollars.

56
00:03:54,607 --> 00:03:56,716
So you gain 50 or lose 100.

57
00:03:56,716 --> 00:04:02,575
Betting pays off on average when 50 p is bigger than 100 times one minus p,

58
00:04:02,575 --> 00:04:04,997
which means p above two thirds.

59
00:04:05,753 --> 00:04:10,499
The regression from that exam predicts about 2.64 million for this golfer,

60
00:04:10,499 --> 00:04:11,397
above the bar.

61
00:04:11,397 --> 00:04:14,540
But that's a predicted amount, not a probability.

62
00:04:14,540 --> 00:04:18,517
You can't compare it with two thirds, so you can't decide yet.

63
00:04:19,287 --> 00:04:24,775
To decide, build a classifier, like a logistic regression on a new column: did

64
00:04:24,775 --> 00:04:26,746
winnings exceed 2.5 million?

65
00:04:26,746 --> 00:04:31,390
Estimate p for this golfer, and bet only if p is above two thirds.

66
00:04:32,153 --> 00:04:38,476
For intuition: actual winnings scatter around the prediction by about 0.89 million.

67
00:04:38,476 --> 00:04:44,265
So the chance of clearing 2.5 million is only around 0.56, below two thirds.

68
00:04:44,265 --> 00:04:49,445
A prediction above the bar is not a probability above the threshold.

69
00:04:51,033 --> 00:04:57,804
To score a model in dollars, multiply each cell's count by its payoff and add.

70
00:04:57,804 --> 00:05:03,186
The model keeps 909 bookings that go fine, at 25 dollars each.

71
00:05:03,186 --> 00:05:06,745
It keeps 26 that cancel, losing 100 each.

72
00:05:06,745 --> 00:05:10,130
And it reassigns 78, worth 14.875 each.

73
00:05:10,130 --> 00:05:13,689
The total is 21,285 dollars and 25 cents.

74
00:05:14,453 --> 00:05:19,108
The baseline that never reassigns earns 978 times 25,

75
00:05:19,108 --> 00:05:22,182
minus 35 times 100: 20,950 dollars.

76
00:05:22,182 --> 00:05:28,418
So the model earns 335 dollars more, even though its accuracy is lower.

77
00:05:28,418 --> 00:05:34,302
Accuracy is the wrong yardstick when errors cost different amounts.

78
00:05:35,900 --> 00:05:40,977
Practice midterm two asks you to justify the losses for Lending Club.

79
00:05:40,977 --> 00:05:46,055
A funded good loan earns 1,000 dollars, a funded default loses 4,000,

80
00:05:46,055 --> 00:05:48,557
and a rejected loan earns nothing.

81
00:05:48,557 --> 00:05:54,297
Funded good loans are true negatives, and funded defaults are false negatives.

82
00:05:54,297 --> 00:05:57,019
So profit is 1,000 TN minus 4,000 FN.

83
00:05:57,787 --> 00:06:03,411
Every actual negative is either a true negative or a false positive,

84
00:06:03,411 --> 00:06:06,140
so TN equals N negative minus FP.

85
00:06:06,140 --> 00:06:10,606
Substitute, and profit becomes 1,000 times N negative,

86
00:06:10,606 --> 00:06:14,080
minus the quantity 4,000 FN plus 1,000 FP.

87
00:06:14,853 --> 00:06:17,855
N negative is fixed by the data.

88
00:06:17,855 --> 00:06:25,171
So maximizing profit is exactly the same as minimizing 4,000 FN plus 1,000 FP.

89
00:06:25,171 --> 00:06:29,204
That's why L_FN is 4,000 and L_FP is 1,000.

90
00:06:30,800 --> 00:06:32,014
Last, ensembles.

91
00:06:32,014 --> 00:06:36,868
If each tree outputs a probability, average the B probabilities,

92
00:06:36,868 --> 00:06:41,040
then compare the average with p*, 0.2 for Lending Club.

93
00:06:41,820 --> 00:06:46,536
But if each tree was trained with the losses 4,000 and 1,000,

94
00:06:46,536 --> 00:06:50,248
its zero or one vote already includes the costs.

95
00:06:50,248 --> 00:06:52,799
So take a simple majority at 0.5.

96
00:06:52,799 --> 00:06:56,201
Using 0.2 there would count the costs twice.

97
00:06:57,800 --> 00:06:58,379
To recap.

98
00:06:58,379 --> 00:07:03,329
A loss is a regret: how much better the right action pays than the wrong one,

99
00:07:03,329 --> 00:07:05,579
in the world that actually happens.

100
00:07:05,579 --> 00:07:10,208
The threshold is L_FP over L_FP plus L_FN, and only their ratio matters.

101
00:07:10,208 --> 00:07:12,780
A predicted amount is not a probability.

102
00:07:12,780 --> 00:07:15,159
Score models by profit, not accuracy.

103
00:07:15,159 --> 00:07:19,209
And when you combine trees, apply p* to averaged probabilities,

104
00:07:19,209 --> 00:07:22,360
but use a simple majority for cost-trained votes.

105
00:07:22,360 --> 00:07:24,096
Next up: linear regression.
